NLP helps machine to understand human language and read it .
  data is basically unstructured nlp help us to make sense of it.
  text processing : raw text is messy , it must be cleaned before any model use it.
    Preprocessing techniques to work more efficiently on our data  
    1.tokenization-breaking text into smaller units/tokens(smallest unit) , 
       ex-sentence into words.
      ex- if paragraph then to in sentence....means depending upon what the downstream task is needed.
    2.lowercase- converting all text to lowercase to avoid ambiquity ....means word look similar capital small doesnot create a difference......so that machine take that particular word always similiar ......
        ex-text:NLP
           clean=text.lower()
    3.removing punctuation- as it is of no use , it is just creating a noice by being their, as adding no meaning .
    4.Stopword removal- removing less helpful words and keeping only more helpful words .
      ex-a,an,the,on,at,this,....article,preposition,pronouns
    5.stemming-to make the machines understand the words convert all into it's root form,using simple rules...removing suffixes...chopping word to root form....
    6.Lemmatization-reducing words to their real dictionary form

nltk-natural language tool kit ....it consist of all grammar and dictionary words which are need to be used...
pip install nltk
import nltk
from nltk.corpus import stopwords
from nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize
from nltk.stem import PorterStemmer
nltk.download('punkt')
nltk.download('punkt_tab')
nltk.download('stopwords')
text='I Enjoy coding.'
print(text)
#tokenization
tokens=word_tokenize(text)
print(tokens)
#stopwordsremoval
stop_words=set(stopwords.words('english'))
print(stop_words)
filtered_words=[]
for word in tokens:
  if word not in stop_words:
    filtered_words.append(word)
    print(word)
#lemmatization
nltk.download('wordnet')
lemmatizer=nltk.WordNetLemmatizer()
tokens=[lemmatizer.lemmatize(word)
for word in filtered_words]
print(tokens)

